<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Deliberately empty at this step.

	  The App Group this extension will eventually need is the thing that
	  requires a registered identifier on the developer account and a
	  provisioning profile that carries it, and adding it here is the single
	  most likely reason a first NSE build fails to sign. Proving the target
	  builds, signs and embeds WITHOUT it first means a signing failure later
	  has exactly one new cause instead of several.

	  NOT `keychain-access-groups`, then or ever: this app's identity is a file
	  in the container, not a Keychain item, and adding the extension to a
	  Keychain access group would change the group of items that already exist
	  — which is how the lock passcode becomes unreadable to the app that wrote
	  it.
	-->
	<!--
	  The one capability this extension does take, and the only way it can read
	  anything the app knows.

	  Byte-identical to the app's entry. A mismatch does not fail the build or
	  the signing; it gives each target its own private container, so the
	  extension opens an empty directory, finds no preference and no armed
	  marker, and falls back to showing nothing. That failure looks exactly
	  like "previews are turned off" and reports itself nowhere.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>group.com.miranatechnologies.tacendum</string>
	</array>
	<!--
	  The one way the platform lets this extension DROP a notification rather
	  than rewrite it: with this entitlement, delivering empty content — no
	  title, no body, no sound — presents nothing at all. It exists for
	  exactly one branch, the blocked-sender guard in NotificationService: a
	  blocked sender's ciphertext still earns a push (blocking is enforced on
	  receipt, deliberately undetectable to them), and without suppression the
	  block was audible — a generic banner and the default sound for every
	  message they cared to fire. jest pins this key
	  (nse.blocked.suppress.test.ts), so removing it is a conscious act.

	  DISTRIBUTION NOTE, in the spirit of the App Group note above: this is
	  an Apple-approved capability. The account holder requests it for the
	  NSE's bundle id at developer.apple.com, and DEVICE signing fails with
	  "provisioning profile doesn't include the … usernotifications.filtering
	  entitlement" until the grant lands in the profile. Simulator builds are
	  unaffected. If a device build is needed before the grant, delete this
	  key AND amend the jest pin together; the Swift's empty delivery still
	  kills the sound and the words on its own — only the bare app-name
	  banner needs the entitlement to go.
	-->
	<!--
	  the key is REMOVED, on the distribution note's own terms.
	  Archiving for the App Store failed exactly as predicted above
	  ("provisioning profile doesn't include the Notification (NSE) Filtering
	  capability"), the capability request to Apple is filed, and shipping was
	  chosen over waiting. Until the grant lands and this key returns, a
	  blocked sender's message presents a bare app-name banner: the empty
	  delivery in NotificationService still strips the sound and every word,
	  and the sender still learns nothing. nse.blocked.suppress.test.ts pins
	  THIS interim state now — re-adding the key must edit that pin back too,
	  so the return is as conscious as the removal.
	-->
</dict>
</plist>
